== 3. d3 ==

White uses the d3-pawn to protect the e-pawn. With their pawn defended, White threatens to capture e5 and win the pawn. This usually compels Black to defend their own pawn. 

The main move is '''3...Nc6''', then Black would like to take advantage of White's passive play with ...d5. Having played d3, White's king's side bishop does not have many places to go. White's principal choices are to play with Be2, leading to a position reminiscent of the [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...d6/3. d4/3...Nd7|Hanham variation of the Philidor]] with colours reversed, or to chose a king's Indian-style set up and play g3 and Bg2. 

Alternatively '''3...d5''' may be played first. After 4. exd5 Qxd5 5. Nc3 Bb4 (pins the knight) 6. Bd2 Bxc3 7. Bxc3 Nc6 (defends e5), Black is even and White again chooses between Be2 and g3 & Bg2. (If 4. Nxe5 dxe4 5. dxe4 Qxd1+ 6. Kxd1 Nxe4 {{Chess/not|-}}.)

