==4. b4!? · Leonhardt gambit==
White gambits a pawn for tempo. The gambit is nearly sound (with best play Black is equal or slightly better) but there are several sharp lines. 

After [[/4...Qxb4|'''4...Qxb4''']], there are two tries for White. 5. Rb1 Qd6 6. Nf3 and White has developed three pieces and has control of the half-open b-file, and its up to White to prove they can capitalise on this in the middle game. 

The other try is 5. Nb5, threatening Nxc7+. 5...Qa5 6. Bc4 sets a trap, where the natural move 6...c6?? loses the queen for two minors: 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 8. Qh5+ g6 9. Nd6+ exd6 10. Qxa5{{chess/not|+++}}. 

Declining the gambit with [[/4...Qb6|'''4...Qb6''']] avoids complications, but by letting White off the hook they don't have to prove their sacrifice was worth it.

=== History ===

It is sometimes called the '''Mieses-Kotrč gambit''', though this seems to be a misnomer. The gambit was first played by [[w:Paul Leonhardt|Paul Leonhardt]] ''against'' [[w:Jacques Mieses|Jacques Mieses]] in 1908,<ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1306501 Leonhardt v Mieses, 1908. Chessgames.com]</ref> and [[w:Jan Kotrč|Jan Kotrč]] is not known to have played it, although 4. b4 is attributed to him in the 7th edition of ''Modern Chess Openings''.<ref>{{Cite book |title=Modern Chess Openings |last=Korn |first=Walter |publisher=David McKay |year=1946 |edition=7 |location=Philadelphia |pages=31 |url=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=uc1.32106012863871&seq=45 |editor-last=Griffith |editor-first=R. C. |editor-last2=Sergeant |editor-first2=P. W.}}</ref>

