== 3. Qxd4!? ==
{{Chess/sideline}}
This move is not the best: though White regains the pawn, Black can harass White's queen with '''3...Nc6'''. Black gets the initiative and nullifies White's first-turn advantage. 

However, 3. Qxd4!? Nc6 is not losing for White: at worst it is an even game, and the continuation 4. Qd3 has been occasionally explored as an anti-theory opening weapon.<ref>e.g. [https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=3030275 Chigaev vs Vakhidov, 2025. Chessgames.com]</ref>

=== History ===
This line was first seen in the 1840s, when Sicilian defence theory was in its infancy. [[w:Howard Staunton|Howard Staunton]] explored this line against [[w:John Cochrane|John Cochrane]] in a match series in 1842.<ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1271842 Staunton v Cochrane, 1842. Chessgames.com]</ref><ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1271843 Staunton v Cochrane, 1842. Chessgames.com]</ref><ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1271846 Staunton v Cochrane, 1842. Chessgames.com]</ref><ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1271855 Staunton v Cochrane, 1842. Chessgames.com]</ref>

