== 4. Bxe4 ==
White recaptures the pawn. Material is level, but the bishop on e4 is exposed and Black can gain a tempo against it. The usual reply is 4...Nf6, developing a knight and attacking the bishop at the same time.

{{BookCat}}

== 4...Nf6 ==
Black develops the king's knight with tempo, forcing the bishop to move again. In the Mieses Attack the bishop normally drops back to f3, where it controls the long diagonal and supports a later g2–g4. Because the bishop occupies f3, White's king's knight is usually developed to e2 or later h3 after an h4 push.

