== 4. Nd2 ==

4. Nd2 is an uncommon sideline in the Caro-Kann advance variation. This is a positional approach. Rather than develop the knight to c3, White plans to route it via d2 to b3, where it will control c5, and White retains the option of playing pawn to c3 or c4. 

'''4...e6''' 5. Nb3 is the main continuation. On b3 the knight controls c5, so if 5...c5 6. dxc5 Bxc5 7. Nxc5 Qa5+ 8. c3 Qxc5 9. Be3 Qc7 10. f4, White gets the bishop pair and some space advantage. Instead, Black may postpone the pawn break and the line may continue 5...Nd7 6. Nf3 Ne7 7. Be2 Nc8 8. O-O Be7. 

The manoeuvre Nd2 & Nb3 is not uncommon in the Caro-Kann. It is also seen in the Short variation, [[../4. Nf3|4. Nf3]], after 4...e6 5. Be2 Nd7 6. O-O Ne7 7. Nbd2 h6 8. Nb3.

=== History ===

This move order was introduced by Yury Vasilchuk in the 1961 Moscow Championship.<ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=2275304 Vasilchuk v Shatskes, 1961. Chessgames.com]</ref><ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=2275293 Vasilchuk v Volovich, 1961. Chessgames.com]</ref> 

By the 2010s it had become fashionable: in Schandorff's book he describes it as "the latest finesse, which is becoming
popular with the trendsetting 2700s."<ref>{{Cite book |title=The Caro-Kann |last=Schandorff |first=Lars |publisher=Quality Chess |year=2010 |isbn=1906552568}}</ref> Recent appearances of 4. Nd2 include at the 2025 World Rapid Championship, where it was played by both [[w:Wesley So|Wesley So]] and [[w:Fabiano Caruana|Fabiano Caruana]].<ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=3029478 So v Amar, 2025. Chessgames.com]</ref><ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=3030243 Caruana v Aravindh, 2025. Chessgames.com]</ref>

